\subsection{A data-computable diagnostic: not supported}
\label{sec:diag}

We tested the diagnostic specified before the runs (Section~\ref{sec:design}) on 6 mechanism groups and 7 graph families (Figure~\ref{fig:diag}; Table~\ref{tab:accept} in Appendix~\ref{app:extra} gives each criterion). \emph{Part (i) is met on its discriminating cells}: where the misfit share is at least 0.2, $L$ beats the best degree benchmark by 0.125 held-out $R^2$ [0.069, 0.318], and the departure of realised variance from $\sigma^2L/n$ has median ratio 1.59 over the 9251 rows of those cells (1.01 over all rows, by construction). The \emph{combined score is not supported}: the pooled held-out gain over the best benchmark is 0.034 [$-0.285$, 0.338] leaving out mechanisms and $-0.009$ [$-0.267$, 0.215] leaving out families; it is positive for 3 of 6 held-out mechanisms (4 are required), and $-0.095$ after deleting the most influential group. For the bias, neither $\bar S$ (0.044 [$-0.009$, 0.084]) nor corner distance times estimated curvature (0.008 [$-0.327$, 0.227]) clears the benchmark.
\begin{figure}[tbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig7_diagnostic.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{A data-computable diagnostic against trivial benchmarks.} (A, B) Held-out predicted against realised log RMSE, one point per cell ($n=2178$), leaving one mechanism out: (A) the combined score, (B) the best degree benchmark. Orange: discriminating set D (heterogeneous degree, cluster designs, real graphs, misspecified rungs); grey: tie cells; solid: $y=x$; dotted: held-out constant prediction. (C) Held-out $R^2$ gain over the best benchmark by held-out group and pooled (diamond), 95\% group-resampling intervals; dashed: threshold 0.05. Blocks: part (i) on D$^*$; the combined score leaving out mechanisms / families. Filled: specified before the runs; open: exploratory $S_{\rm adj}$.}
\label{fig:diag}
\end{figure}

The gains must be read next to absolute held-out $R^2$ of log $|{\rm bias}|$: on the 1224 cells where the population-OLS prediction exists, every data-computable predictor has a negative absolute held-out $R^2$ ($\bar S$ $-0.22$, noise-adjusted $S$ $-0.06$, corner times curvature $-0.12$, the best degree benchmark $p(1-p)$ $-0.26$; a constant, the training mean, $-0.24$), so $\bar S$ and the benchmark are within 0.03 of a constant, while the population-OLS prediction, which needs the true response, reaches 0.987. The exploratory noise-adjusted $S$ gains 0.20 [0.10, 0.30] over a degree benchmark that is no better than a constant. We conclude that these data give \emph{no evidence of a useful data-computable bias score}, which is not evidence that none exists: the intervals include both. A reading that fits the other results is that the bias is determined by the response at the corners, which data inside the observed exposure range do not identify; this is an interpretation, not a separate test. The score also does not flag a wrong feature graph: with 10\% and 30\% of edges missing the bias moves from 0 to $-0.057$ and $-0.162$ while $S$ falls from 0.273 to 0.217 (Appendix~\ref{app:extra}).
